 "notes": "24 facets = UTC -11 ... +12 (de-wiki: 24-sided drum, one side per main time zone; post-1997 grouping per hu-wiki and 2017 photo transcription). Each facet has an upper plate (above the rotating hour ring) and a lower plate (below it); 'cities_1989' = upper list then lower list, each read top-to-bottom as cut. Spellings are as cut on the plates (German exonyms, all caps). '+' / '(+30')' marks half-hour zones (en-wiki: 'NEW DELHI +30`'). Facet order: UTC increases left-to-right as seen from outside, i.e. counter-clockwise seen from above (all photos agree). order_on_drum counts from the UTC+1 (Berlin) facet in increasing-UTC direction. The '1989' readings come from photos dated 15 Jul 1980 (Bundesarchiv), Apr 1984 (Bundesarchiv), 30 Aug 1989 (Bundesarchiv) and a c.1991 colour photo (Commons 'Berlin Alexanderplatz Kaufhof', caption 1991; shows LENINGRAD, GORKI, SWERDLOWSK, ALMA-ATA so it is unambiguously pre-1997). Assumption: no plate changes between Nov 1989 and the c.1991 photo (no works documented before Oct-Dec 1997). Plates WERE changed between 1970 and 1980 and again between 1980 and c.1991 (consistent with a reported 1985 maintenance), so 1970s photos are NOT a substitute for 1989; they are given only under 'earlier_states'. 1997 renovation (Oct-Dec 1997, 350,000 DM): 20 names added (documented examples: Oslo, Kapstadt, Tel Aviv, Jerusalem, Seoul, Istanbul), renames (Leningrad->St. Petersburg, Alma-Ata->Almaty, plus photo-evidenced Gorki->Nischnij Nowgorod, Swerdlowsk->Jekaterinburg, Tbilissi->Tiflis, Jerewan->Eriwan, Aschchabad->Aschgabat, Delhi->New Delhi, Dacca->Dhaka, Saná->Sanaa, Phoengjang->Pjöngjang), Kiew moved +3->+2, and photo-evidenced removals of ALGIER, BRAZZAVILLE, LUANDA, KHARTUM, LUSAKA, MAPUTO. Counts disagree across sources: official site says 80 original names (1969) and 146 now; Erich John (B.Z. 2019) 97 original / 148; Tagesspiegel 2019 caption 147; de-wiki 146 + date-line entry.",
 "completeness": "7 of 24 facets have a full pre-1997 (c.1989-91) list read from period photos (UTC 0, +1..+6; +3 bottom has one illegible 9th line). +7..+12: upper plates verified (1984/1989 photos), lower plates unknown or partial (+8 starts PEKING, SHANGHAI; +9 starts PHOENGJANG). -6..-2: best available is a c.1978 reading (marked reconstructed; may have changed in the 1985 maintenance). -11..-8 and -1: no usable post-1980 photo; only 1971 states and post-1997 lists. About 80 names are directly verified from 1980-1991 photos (70 on the 7 complete facets + ~11 on upper plates / first lines of +7..+12), out of an estimated ~130 pre-1997 names (146 now - 20 added in 1997 + at least 6 removed). The post-1997 list (146 names + DATUMSGRENZE) is complete.",
 "orientation": "No source documents the compass orientation of the facets; nothing found says which facet faces east-northeast. The drum and plates are fixed; only the hour ring and the planetary model rotate. Rough photo-based estimate (subject to large uncertainty, +/-2 facets): photos looking toward the Fernsehturm (which lies WSW of the clock, bearing ~263 deg) show the +7..+10 facets frontal (Apr 1984, 30 Aug 1989), and photos looking north toward the Kaufhof/Centrum store show +3/+4 frontal. Since UTC increases counter-clockwise seen from above (facet bearing drops ~15 deg per +1 h), this implies the Berlin (+1) facet faces roughly S-SW (~175-235 deg) and the facet facing ENE (67.5 deg) is one of +8..+12 (Ulan Bator/Irkutsk ... Kamtschatka), most likely around +10/+11 (CHABAROWSK/WLADIWOSTOK or MAGADAN/SACHALIN). The pavement compass rose (stone mosaic, de-wiki) would settle this on site; this needs a compass bearing taken at the clock or a georeferenced photo of the drum.",
 "other_features": {
  "planetary_model": "Simplified solar-system sculpture of spheres (planets) on steel-ring orbits above the drum; rotates once per minute (de-wiki citing Erich John). Photos show a ringed sphere (Saturn). Total height 10 m, mass 16 t.",
  "hour_ring": "Rotating ring with numerals 1-24 in colour-coded rectangles (film-strip style frames in 1970s photos); colours reportedly follow Goethe's colour theory (hu-wiki citing kreuzberged.com 2017). Since 1997 the motor runs 5% fast and stops on hourly cams synchronised to DCF77 (Mainflingen).",
  "column": "2.7 m column, 1.5 m diameter, tiled with a world map, four small analogue clocks around it (en-wiki; visible in 1970 and Aug 1989 photos).",
  "compass_rose": "Stone mosaic in the form of a compass rose (Windrose) set into the pavement under the column (de-wiki). No text documented.",
  "base_inscriptions": "No inscription texts on the base were found in any source. The only text elements documented are the city names, the half-hour '+30'' marks and the 'DATUMSGRENZE' (date-line) entry.",
  "dates": "Inaugurated 30 Sep 1969 (some sources: 7 Oct 1969); designer Erich John; metalwork Hans-Joachim Kunsch; protected monument since July 2015."
 },
